<BaseLayout title="Hecatoncheires">
  <h1>Hecatoncheires</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    A governance and observability plane for autonomous AI agent fleets on AWS. It gives agents
    that call Amazon Bedrock an identity, watches what they do with it, and can pull their
    permissions when they misbehave.
  </p>

  <h2>The problem</h2>

  <p>
    An autonomous agent, with no obstructive human in the loop, acts faster than a person can
    review and decides its own next step. Conventional systems don't. You can read the code and
    enumerate what a service will do; you cannot read an agent and enumerate what it will choose,
    with what input, in what sequence, and that is where the cost overruns and the security
    incidents live. An agent fleet needs a governance plane that runs at the agent's speed rather
    than a human's.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works, roughly</h2>

  <p>
    The approach rests on three AWS primitives: inference profiles give each agent an identity,
    invocation logging gives you a record of what it did, and IAM enforces what it's allowed to
    do. A standardized framework shapes, grants, and revokes those permissions, and a circuit
    breaker can revoke them wholesale when an agent runs away.
  </p>

  <h2>Any agent, as long as it's Bedrock</h2>

  <p>
    The core design commitment is agnostic agent ops. An agent's internals are its own business,
    and I'm not about to track every popular framework's RSS feed just to keep this thing running.
    The moment an agent reaches outside itself into your AWS estate, there's a common control
    point, and it's IAM, which along with the other two primitives has no deeper concept of what
    kind of agent it's governing, so the same machinery is built to govern an AWS-hosted agent
    loop, an external agent assuming a role, or a container running custom code, without needing to
    know the difference. We'll still label it for our own purposes, of course.
  </p>

  <h2>The name</h2>

  <p>
    The Hecatoncheires were three giants, each with a hundred hands, the Greek gods called on to
    put down the Titans and then guard them, chained, in Tartarus. The platform is named for those
    hundred hands, built to cradle and watch over a fleet of these modern Titans, our agents, and
    to halt them when it has to. The name is deliberately a bit wacky. This isn't meant to be a
    brand.
  </p>

  <h2>Status and the series</h2>

  <p>
    The basic constructs and structure are broadly code-complete; I'm now deploying, testing, and
    verifying the theory, getting close to calling the core shareable before I either go deeper into
    the build or make it easier for folks to sample the pieces they need. For more, look at the
    <a href="/hecatoncheires/blog/">series</a> to the left or dig through the
    <a href="https://github.com/MikeORed/hecatoncheires" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">repository</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
